<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rebellion of the Three Guards</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1040 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Rebellion of the Three Monitors</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rebellion of the Three Guards was a revolt early in the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> by three brothers of King Wu, Guan Shu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-shu-du">Cai Shu</a> and Huo Shu, who had been set to watch the conquered Shang. Allied with the Shang prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-geng">Wu Geng</a>, they rose against the regency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a>, who crushed them after about three years and redistributed the Shang heartland among loyal kin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-three-guards.jpg" alt="Rebellion of the Three Guards" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: File:EN-WesternZhouStates.jpg: Philg88 derivative work: Applodion · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rebellion_of_the_Three_Guards.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
